 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Requires the Hudson River Black River Regulating District to undertake a comprehensive study regarding the beneficiaries of the district and real property tax apportionments to establish a standard methodology for the determination of any future apportionment.",
 "title": "Requires the Hudson River Black River Regulating District to undertake a comprehensive study regarding the beneficiaries of the district and real property tax apportionments",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A10070",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A10070-2017",
     "printNo": "A10070",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 292\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 10070-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n      requiring the Hudson River  Black  River  Regulating  District  to\n      undertake a comprehensive study regarding the beneficiaries of the\n      district and real property tax apportionments to establish a stan-\n      dard  methodology  for  the determination of any future apportion-\n      ment\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend the  Environmental  Conservation  Law  (ECL)  to\nrequire  the Hudson River Black River Regulating District (the District)\nto conduct a comprehensive study of  the  District's  beneficiaries  and\nreal  property  tax apportionments to establish a methodology for appor-\ntionment of the District's costs. The bill would require the District to\ndeliver a report to the Governor, State Comptroller and the  Legislature\nno  later than January 1, 2020 and submit annual reports to the Governor\nalong with the district's three-year budget.\n \n  The current law, ECL Article 15, Title 21, already provides a  compre-\nhensive  process  by which the District must establish a budget covering\nthe costs of land acquisition, dam  construction,  and  ongoing  mainte-\nnance,  and then apportion such costs to offset budgeted expenditures to\npublic corporations  and  parcels  of  real  estate  benefited  by  such\nprojects.  Moreover,  the  existing  process already provides a means to\nmodify the apportionment when appropriate. This process includes  notice\nas  well as public hearings, and is reviewable as a final agency action.\nSince those affected by the apportionment process  have  notice  of  the\nassessment  and  apportionment process, and the existing methodology has\nbeen vetted through the courts, the use of State resources for  a  study\nand report is inefficient. Moreover, the costs that would necessarily be\nincurred  by  the  District  to re-evaluate its own processes would only\nincrease the costs to those entities the report would  presumably  bene-\nfit.\n \n  The bill would also require the District to evaluate real property tax\napportionments  in  the  study  and include the result of the District's\nre-evaluation in the report by January 1, 2020. In  light  of  the  fact\nthat  existing  law  squarely  addresses  the  tax  obligations  of  the\nDistrict, and the fact that the District does not have the authority  to\ncontrol  how District property is assessed and taxed, this aspect of the\nbill is unwarranted. For those reasons, I am constrained  to  veto  this\nbill.\n \nThe bill is disapproved.                      (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
